is describe still a valid way to view the results of the plot

Yes, .describe() is still a completely valid way to view summary statistics, but with one important catch: it does not calculate the box plot whiskers or explicitly identify outliers for you.

Here is how .describe() compares to what your box plot actually shows:

What .describe() gives you directly
Median: Listed directly as 50%.

Q1 and Q3: Listed directly as 25% and 75%.

Interquartile Range (IQR): Easily calculated as 75% minus 25%.

What .describe() DOES NOT give you directly
Whisker Endpoints:

The min and max values in .describe() represent the absolute extreme values in your dataset, not the ends of the box plot whiskers.

On a standard box plot, whiskers extend to the most extreme data points within 1.5×IQR. .describe() does not compute these boundary cutoffs for you.

Outliers:

.describe() will not tell you how many outliers exist or what their specific values are.

If your max value is much higher than Q3+1.5×IQR, you know an outlier exists, but you won't know if it's a single point or a group of fifty.

Comparison Summary
Metric	Box Plot / boxplot_stats()	.describe()
Median	Yes (50%)	Yes (50%)
Q1 / Q3	Yes (25% / 75%)	Yes (25% / 75%)
Outliers	Explicitly identified (fliers)	Hidden within min / max range
Whiskers	Explicitly calculated (whislo / whishi)	Not calculated (shows absolute min / max)
Bottom line: Use .describe() for a quick 5-number summary of the distribution, but use boxplot_stats() or your plot when you specifically need to isolate, count, or filter out box plot outliers.

