sns.heatmap(
    corr_matrix, 
    annot=True,          # Display numeric values inside cells
    fmt=".2f",           # Format numbers to 2 decimal places
    cmap='coolwarm',     # Red for positive, blue for negative correlation
    vmin=-1, vmax=1,     # Fix colorbar limits between -1 and +1
    center=0,            # Center colorbar at 0
    linewidths=0.5
)
plt.title("Correlation Matrix Heatmap")
plt.show()
Practical Applications
Feature Selection (Predictive Modeling): Identify features that correlate strongly with your target variable to prioritize them during model building.

Detecting Multicollinearity: Uncover independent features that have high mutual correlation (e.g., >∣0.80∣). Redundant variables can cause instability or inflated variances in regression models.

Data Quality Checks: Identify unexpected relationships or verify domain-knowledge assumptions early in exploratory data analysis (EDA).

Limitations to Keep in Mind
Linearity Only: Standard correlation (Pearson) only captures linear relationships. A non-linear relationship (e.g., quadratic or U-shaped) might yield a correlation close to zero despite a strong actual pattern.

Outlier Sensitivity: Extreme outlier values can heavily distort correlation coefficients.

